    G0_7 [label="X . .\n. . .\n. O ."];
    G0_8 [label="X . .\n. . .\n. . O"];

    C0 -> G0_1;
    C0 -> G0_2;
    C0 -> G0_3;
    C0 -> G0_4;
    C0 -> G0_5;
    C0 -> G0_6;
    C0 -> G0_7;
    C0 -> G0_8;

    // The 8 successors of C1
    G1_0 [label="O X .\n. . .\n. . ."];
    G1_2 [label=". X O\n. . .\n. . ."];
    G1_3 [label=". X .\nO . .\n. . ."];
    G1_4 [label=". X .\n. O .\n. . ."];
    G1_5 [label=". X .\n. . O\n. . ."];
    G1_6 [label=". X .\n. . .\nO . ."];
    G1_7 [label=". X .\n. . .\n. O ."];
    G1_8 [label=". X .\n. . .\n. . O"];

    C1 -> G1_0;
    C1 -> G1_2;
    C1 -> G1_3;
    C1 -> G1_4;
    C1 -> G1_5;
    C1 -> G1_6;
    C1 -> G1_7;
    C1 -> G1_8;
}
```

* Redundant Paths and Graph Search

::: columns
:::: {.column width=55%}
- @Definition@: three kinds of "wasted work" while searching a search tree
  - **Loopy path**: a path revisiting a state already on that same path
    - Never better than the same path with the loop removed
  - **Repeated path**: a loopy path of length two, from undoing the last
    reversible action
    - E.g. driving from A to B and immediately back to A
    - Special (but common) case of a loopy path when there are reversible actions
  - **Redundant path**: any case where several paths join the same two states

::::
:::: {.column width=45%}

```graphviz
digraph WastedWork {
    bgcolor="transparent";
    pad="0.15";
    splines=spline;
    nodesep=0.25;
    ranksep=0.35;
    rankdir=TB;

    node [shape=circle, style="filled", penwidth=1.8,
          fontname="Helvetica", fontsize=11, width=0.38, fixedsize=true,
          fillcolor="#C7ECF0", color="#7CC6D0", fontcolor="#1F4E56"];
    edge [color="#A3B1C0", penwidth=1.3, arrowhead=vee, arrowsize=0.7];

    // green = start state | cyan = other state | red edge = the wasted work

    subgraph cluster_loopy {
        label     = "Loopy";
        labelloc  = "t";
        fontname  = "Helvetica-Bold";
        fontsize  = 11;
        fontcolor = "#3A4A5C";
        style     = "rounded";
        color     = "#C7D0DA";
        margin    = 12;

        LA [label="A", fillcolor="#A9DDB0", color="#4F9A5C",
            fontcolor="#1F4E2E"];
        LB [label="B"];
        LC [label="C"];

        LA -> LB;
        LB -> LC;
        LC -> LA [color="#C0455B", penwidth=1.6, constraint=false];
    }

    subgraph cluster_repeated {
        label     = "Repeated";
        labelloc  = "t";
        fontname  = "Helvetica-Bold";
        fontsize  = 11;
        fontcolor = "#3A4A5C";
        style     = "rounded";
        color     = "#C7D0DA";
        margin    = 12;

        RA [label="A", fillcolor="#A9DDB0", color="#4F9A5C",
            fontcolor="#1F4E2E"];
        RB [label="B"];

        RA -> RB;
        RB -> RA [color="#C0455B", penwidth=1.6, constraint=false];
    }

    subgraph cluster_redundant {
        label     = "Redundant";
        labelloc  = "t";
        fontname  = "Helvetica-Bold";
        fontsize  = 11;
        fontcolor = "#3A4A5C";
        style     = "rounded";
        color     = "#C7D0DA";
        margin    = 12;

        DA [label="A", fillcolor="#A9DDB0", color="#4F9A5C",
            fontcolor="#1F4E2E"];
        DB [label="B"];
        DC [label="C"];
        DD [label="D"];

        DA -> DB;
        DA -> DC;
        DB -> DD [color="#C0455B", penwidth=1.6];
        DC -> DD [color="#C0455B", penwidth=1.6];
    }
}
```

::::
:::

* Redundant Paths and Graph Search: Example

::: columns
:::: {.column width=70%}
- Graph search for tic-tac-toe suffers only from _redundant paths_
  - No mark is ever removed, so no path can loop or repeat
- E.g.,
  - `X` plays 0, then `O` plays 1, then `X` 4 
  - `X` plays 4, then `O` plays 1, then `X` 0
  - Both reach the same board
::::
:::: {.column width=30%}

```graphviz
digraph ttt_redundant_paths {
    bgcolor="transparent";
    pad="0.15";
    rankdir=TB;
    splines=spline;
    nodesep=0.35;
    ranksep=0.40;

    node [shape=box, style="rounded,filled", penwidth=1.2,
          fontname="Courier", fontsize=9, margin="0.06,0.05"];
    edge [color="#A3B1C0", penwidth=1.0, arrowhead=vee, arrowsize=0.6,
          fontname="Helvetica", fontsize=9, fontcolor="#7B8794"];

    // green = initial state | cyan = intermediate state | blue = shared final state

    S0 [label=". . .\n. . .\n. . .", fillcolor="#A9DDB0",
        color="#4F9A5C", fontcolor="#1F4E2E"];

    L1 [label="X . .\n. . .\n. . .", fillcolor="#C7ECF0",
        color="#7CC6D0", fontcolor="#1F4E56"];
    L2 [label="X O .\n. . .\n. . .", fillcolor="#C7ECF0",
        color="#7CC6D0", fontcolor="#1F4E56"];

    R1 [label=". . .\n. X .\n. . .", fillcolor="#C7ECF0",
        color="#7CC6D0", fontcolor="#1F4E56"];
    R2 [label=". O .\n. X .\n. . .", fillcolor="#C7ECF0",
        color="#7CC6D0", fontcolor="#1F4E56"];

    Final [label="X O .\n. X .\n. . .", fillcolor="#9CC4F2",
           color="#3C6FB0", fontcolor="#1F4E79"];

    S0 -> L1 [label="  X:0  "];
    L1 -> L2 [label="  O:1  "];
    L2 -> Final [label="  X:4  ", color="#C0455B", penwidth=1.6];

    S0 -> R1 [label="  X:4  "];
    R1 -> R2 [label="  O:1  "];
    R2 -> Final [label="  X:0  ", color="#C0455B", penwidth=1.6];
}
```
::::
:::

* Redundant Paths and Graph Search

- @Problem@
  - Without bookkeeping the search re-expands a state every time a path reaches it
  - E.g., reaching the opposite corner of a $d \times d$ grid takes
    $\frac{(2d)!}{(d!)^2}$ distinct paths over only $(d + 1)^2$ states

// TODO(gp): Consider a plot to illustrate the above

- @Solution@: graph search keeps an **explored set** of expanded states
  - Aka "closed list"
  - Expand a state the first time it is seen, or again only when a cheaper path to it
    appears
  - _Cons_: memory now grows with the number of _states_ reached

- @Remark@: the frontier splits the state space into an explored and an unexplored
  region
  - _Invariant_: every path from $s_0$ to an unexplored state passes through some
    frontier state

- @Definition@: **expanding** a node means generating all its successors
  - Cost is counted in node expansions, so the analysis is machine independent
  - A **leaf node** is a node with no children yet (not expanded yet)

* Tic-Tac-Toe: Optimizing the Search Tree

::: columns
:::: {.column width=60%}
- @Key idea@: only 3 of the 9 successors are distinct once the symmetries of the
  square are removed
  - Rotations and reflections map a corner to a corner and an edge to an edge
  - Searching a corner, an edge, and the center covers the whole first ply
::::
:::: {.column width=40%}

```graphviz
digraph TicTacToeFirstPly {
    bgcolor="transparent";
    pad="0.15";
    rankdir=TB;
    splines=spline;
    nodesep=0.45;
    ranksep=0.55;

    node [shape=box, style="rounded,filled", penwidth=1.6,
          fontname="Courier", fontsize=13, margin="0.18,0.12"];
    edge [color="#A3B1C0", penwidth=1.3, arrowhead=vee, arrowsize=0.75,
          fontname="Helvetica", fontsize=11, fontcolor="#7B8794"];

    // green = initial state | blue = state reached after one action

    S0 [label=". . .\n. . .\n. . .",
        fillcolor="#A9DDB0", color="#4F9A5C", fontcolor="#1F4E2E"];

    Corner [label="X . .\n. . .\n. . .",
            fillcolor="#9CC4F2", color="#3C6FB0", fontcolor="#1F4E79"];
    Side   [label=". X .\n. . .\n. . .",
            fillcolor="#9CC4F2", color="#3C6FB0", fontcolor="#1F4E79"];
    Center [label=". . .\n. X .\n. . .",
            fillcolor="#9CC4F2", color="#3C6FB0", fontcolor="#1F4E79"];

    S0 -> Corner [label="  Corner  "];
    S0 -> Side   [label="  Edge  "];
    S0 -> Center [label="  Center  "];
}
```
::::
:::

- @Remark@: the same reduction applies deeper in the tree
  - Fewer symmetries survive as the board fills up
  - There is a symmetry between `X` and `O` swapping the roles

* Measuring a Search Algorithm
// From: msml610/lectures_source/Lesson09.9-MonteCarlo_Tree_Search.smd:32 '* Why Search Trees Explode'
// TODO(gp): Copy / move / merge some content from 9.8 here

- @Definition@: four **criteria evaluate a search algorithm**
  - _Completeness_: is the algorithm guaranteed to find a solution, if one
    exists?
  - _Optimality_: if it finds a solution, is that solution optimal?
  - _Time complexity_: how long does it take to find a solution?
  - _Space complexity_: how much memory does it need?

- @Problem@:
  - The _search graph_ is given implicitly by $s_0$, $Actions$, and $Result$
    - It is often infinite (even if state space $|V|, |E|$ is finite)

* Measuring a Search Algorithm

- @Components@: the parameters the complexities are written in
  - Branching factor $b$: the maximum or average number of successors of a node
  - Depth of the shallowest goal node $d$
  - Maximum depth of any path, possibly infinite $m$

```{=typst}
#styled-table(
  headers: ([Domain], [Branching $b$], [Depth], [Tree size]),
  rows: (
    ([Tic-tac-toe], [$approx 5$], [$9$], [$10^5$]),
    ([Connect 4], [$approx 7$], [$42$], [$10^35$]),
    ([Chess], [$approx 35$], [$approx 80$], [$10^123$]),
    ([Go, 19x19 board], [$approx 250$], [$approx 150$], [$10^360$]),
    ([Robot task planning], [$10^2 - 10^4$], [$10 - 100$], [intractable]),
  ),
  col-widths: (1.8fr, 1fr, 1fr, 1fr),
  bold-first-col: true
)
```

- @Remark@: the growth is in the _exponent_, so faster hardware (even $1000$x
  more compute) buys almost nothing
  - There are around $10^{80}$ atoms in the visible universe

- @Example@: Connect 4 has $b \approx 7$ and $m = 42$, so $b^m \approx 10^{35}$
  - Only $4.5 \cdot 10^{12}$ of those sequences reach distinct legal positions

- @Problem@: memory, not time, is often the binding constraint in practice
  - E.g., a machine generating $10^6$ nodes/s fills $100$ GB of RAM in minutes

# Uninformed Search

* Uninformed Search

- @Definition@: **uninformed search** has only the definition of the problem,
  and no other problem-specific knowledge
  - Aka "blind search"

::: columns
:::: {.column width=50%}

- @Key idea@: 
  - Uninformed search algorithms differ only in the strategy to pick _the next
    node_
  - Every algorithm in this lesson is **best-first search** run with a different
    evaluation function $f(n)$

::::
:::: {.column width=50%}

```{=typst}
#styled-table(
  headers: ([Evaluation $f(n)$], [Algorithm]),
  rows: (
    ([$"depth"(n)$], [Breadth-first]),
    ([$g(n)$], [Uniform-cost]),
    ([$h(n)$], [Greedy best-first]),
    ([$g(n) + h(n)$], [A\* search]),
  ),
  col-widths: (1.7fr, 1.3fr),
  bold-first-col: true
)
```

::::
:::

* Best-First Search: Algorithm

- @Algorithm@: best-first search
  1. Initialize the frontier with $s_0$, and the explored set to empty
  2. Pop the node $n$ of lowest $f(n)$ from the frontier
  3. Return the path to $n$ if $IsGoal(n)$ holds
  4. Otherwise move $n$ to the explored set and expand it, adding each successor that
     is new or reached more cheaply than before
  5. Repeat until the frontier is empty

```graphviz[width=75%]
digraph best_first_loop {
    bgcolor="transparent";
    pad="0.15";
    rankdir=LR;
    nodesep=0.35;
    ranksep=0.55;

    node [shape=box, style="rounded,filled", penwidth=1.4,
          fontname="Helvetica", fontsize=11, margin="0.14,0.09"];
    edge [color="#A3B1C0", penwidth=1.2, arrowhead=vee, arrowsize=0.7,
          fontname="Helvetica", fontsize=10, fontcolor="#7B8794"];

    // green = init | cyan = pop/test | orange = expand | blue = terminal outcome

    Init [label="Initialize\nfrontier", fillcolor="#A9DDB0",
          color="#4F9A5C", fontcolor="#1F4E2E"];
    Pop  [label="Pop lowest\nf(n) node n", fillcolor="#C7ECF0",
          color="#7CC6D0", fontcolor="#1F4E56"];
    Test [label="IsGoal(n)?", shape=diamond, fillcolor="#C7ECF0",
          color="#7CC6D0", fontcolor="#1F4E56"];
    Expand [label="Expand n,\nadd successors", fillcolor="#FFC98A",
            color="#D98E2B", fontcolor="#6B4517"];
    Return [label="Return path to n", fillcolor="#9CC4F2",
            color="#3C6FB0", fontcolor="#1F4E79"];

    Init -> Pop;
    Pop -> Test;
    Test -> Return [label="  yes  "];
    Test -> Expand [label="  no  "];
    Expand -> Pop [label="  repeat  ", constraint=false];
}
```

// TODO(gp): Maybe convert it into a table on the side
- @Components@: the frontier data structure selects the strategy
  - FIFO queue gives breadth-first
  - LIFO stack gives depth-first
  - Priority queue gives best-first

* Breadth-First

::: columns
:::: {.column width=70%}
- @Definition@: **Breadth-first search (BFS)** expands every node at a given
  depth before moving to the next level, so $f(n) = depth(n)$
  - A FIFO queue replaces the priority queue, since depth grows by one per
    expansion
  - The goal test fires when a node is _generated_, so the search stops early

- @Properties@: BFS finds the shallowest goal node
  - _Complete_ whenever $b$ is finite
  - _Optimal_ only when every edge carries the same cost
  - _Time_ and _space_ are both $O(b^d)$, since the whole frontier is stored

- @Problem@: with _unequal step_ costs the shallowest goal is not the cheapest
  - E.g., a direct road of cost $100$ is returned before a three-hop route of
    three edges costing $10$ each (with total cost $30$)
::::
:::: {.column width=30%}

```graphviz
digraph bfs_tree {
    bgcolor="transparent";
    pad="0.15";
    rankdir=TB;
    splines=line;
    nodesep=0.30;
    ranksep=0.40;

    node [shape=circle, style="filled", penwidth=1.4,
          fontname="Helvetica", fontsize=10, width=0.55, fixedsize=true];
    edge [color="#A3B1C0", penwidth=1.1, arrowhead=vee, arrowsize=0.6];

    S0 [label="S0\n#1", fillcolor="#A9DDB0", color="#4F9A5C", fontcolor="#1F4E2E"];
    A  [label="A\n#2",  fillcolor="#C7ECF0", color="#7CC6D0", fontcolor="#1F4E56"];
    B  [label="B\n#3",  fillcolor="#C7ECF0", color="#7CC6D0", fontcolor="#1F4E56"];
    C  [label="C\n#4",  fillcolor="#9CC4F2", color="#3C6FB0", fontcolor="#1F4E79"];
    D  [label="D\n#5",  fillcolor="#9CC4F2", color="#3C6FB0", fontcolor="#1F4E79"];
    E  [label="E\n#6",  fillcolor="#9CC4F2", color="#3C6FB0", fontcolor="#1F4E79"];
    G  [label="G\n#7",  fillcolor="#9CC4F2", color="#C0455B", penwidth=2.2,
        fontcolor="#1F4E79"];

    S0 -> A;
    S0 -> B;
    A -> C;
    A -> D;
    B -> E;
    B -> G;
}
```

```graphviz[width=70%]
digraph bfs_unequal_cost {
    bgcolor="transparent";
    pad="0.15";
    rankdir=TB;
    splines=line;
    nodesep=0.20;
    ranksep=0.30;

    node [shape=circle, style="filled", penwidth=1.4,
          fontname="Helvetica", fontsize=10, width=0.55, fixedsize=true];
    edge [color="#A3B1C0", penwidth=1.1, arrowhead=vee, arrowsize=0.6,
          fontname="Helvetica", fontsize=9, fontcolor="#7B8794"];

    // G appears twice: same goal state, reached via two different search-tree paths

    S0 [label="S0", fillcolor="#A9DDB0", color="#4F9A5C", fontcolor="#1F4E2E"];
    M1 [label="M1", fillcolor="#C7ECF0", color="#7CC6D0", fontcolor="#1F4E56"];
    M2 [label="M2", fillcolor="#C7ECF0", color="#7CC6D0", fontcolor="#1F4E56"];
    G1 [label="G", fillcolor="#9CC4F2",
        color="#C0455B", penwidth=2.2, fontcolor="#1F4E79", fixedsize=false];
    G2 [label="G", fillcolor="#9CC4F2",
        color="#3C6FB0", fontcolor="#1F4E79", fixedsize=false];

    S0 -> G1 [label="  cost 100  ", color="#C0455B", penwidth=1.6,
              fontcolor="#C0455B"];
    S0 -> M1 [label="  cost 10  "];
    M1 -> M2 [label="  cost 10  "];
    M2 -> G2 [label="  cost 10  "];
}
```
::::
:::

* Uniform-Cost Search

- @Definition@: **Uniform-cost search** orders nodes by the lowest path
  cost $g(n)$, so $f(n) = g(n)$
  - Aka "Dijkstra's algorithm"
  - It is breadth-first search using path cost instead of number of edges
  - The goal test fires on _expansion_, so a cheaper path can still overtake a
    goal sitting on the frontier

- @Properties@:
  - Uniform-cost search is _complete_ and _optimal_ for non-negative step costs
  - _Time_ and _space_ are $O(b^{1 + C^* / c_{min}})$, with $C^*$ the optimal cost
    and $c_{min}$ the smallest step cost

* Depth-First Search

- @Definition@: **Depth-first search (DFS)** always expands the deepest node in the
  frontier
  - It descends until a node has no successors, then backs up to the next
    deepest node with unexplored successors
  - A LIFO stack replaces the priority queue (or plain recursion does)
  - _Space_ is only $O(bm)$, since one path plus its siblings is stored

- @Cons@: DFS is _neither complete nor optimal_
  - A single infinite branch traps it forever
  - The first goal found is rarely the shallowest

* More Variants

- @Definition@: **depth-limited search (DLS)** runs DFS but refuses to expand past a
  fixed depth $\ell$
  - _Time_ $O(b^\ell)$ and _space_ $O(b\ell)$
  - _Cons_: _complete_ only if $\ell \geq d$ (so need to know $d$ in advance)

- @Definition@: **iterative deepening search (IDS)** runs DLS for
  $\ell = 0, 1, 2, \ldots$ until a goal appears
  - _Pros_
    - No need to know $d$ in advance
    - DFS's low memory $O(bd)$
    - BFS's completeness/optimality (if equal costs)
  - _Cons_
    - Redundant expansion of shallow leaves (but the exponential complexity is
      deep in the tree)

- @Definition@: **bidirectional search** runs one search forward from $s_0$, one
  backward from the goal, hoping they meet in the middle
  - _Pros_: $b^{d/2} + b^{d/2}$ is far smaller than $b^d$
  - _Cons_: it needs a way to search backwards, and the two frontiers must be
    compared at every step

* Uninformed Search: Comparison

```{=typst}
#styled-table(
  headers: ([Algorithm], [Complete], [Optimal], [Time], [Space]),
  rows: (
    ([Breadth-first], [Yes], [Equal costs only], [$O(b^d)$], [$O(b^d)$]),
    ([Uniform-cost], [Yes], [Yes], [$O(b^(1 + C^* \/ c_"min"))$], [Same as time]),
    ([Depth-first], [No], [No], [$O(b^m)$], [$O(b m)$]),
    ([Depth-limited], [If $ell >= d$], [No], [$O(b^ell)$], [$O(b ell)$]),
    ([Iterative deepening], [Yes], [Equal costs only], [$O(b^d)$], [$O(b d)$]),
  ),
  col-widths: (2fr, 1fr, 1.7fr, 1.3fr, 1.7fr),
  bold-first-col: true
)
```

- @Key idea@: iterative deepening is the default uninformed method
  - It matches BFS on solution quality at DFS memory cost

- @Problem@: every entry is still exponential in the solution depth
  - No ordering of _blind_ expansions escapes $b^d$, because a goal can hide
    below any node not yet expanded

- @Solution@: inject problem-specific knowledge as a **heuristic**, so the
  search stops treating all frontier nodes as equally promising

# Informed Search

* Heuristics

- @Definition@: **informed search** uses _problem-specific knowledge_ to find a
  solution more efficiently than an _uninformed strategy_
  - Aka "heuristic search"
  - The knowledge enters through the ranking of _"more promising"_ nodes

- @Definition@: a **heuristic function** $h(n)$ estimates the cheapest cost
  from the state at node $n$ to a goal state
  - $h$ is problem specific
  - $h(n) = 0$ when $n$ is a goal state
  - $h(n) \geq 0$ at every node

// TODO(ai_gp): Add a graphviz diagram with n -> Goal with \approx h(n)

- @Key idea@: informed search buys speed with knowledge
  - The quality of the heuristic $h$, not the choice of search algorithm, decides how
    much is saved

* Heuristics

- @Components@: the three functions used from here on
  - $g(n)$: the path cost of $n$ from the initial state (known exactly)
  - $h(n)$: the _estimated_ cost from $n$ to a goal (cheap to computed)
  - $f(n)$: the order in which nodes are expanded
      - $f(n) = g(n) + h(n)$ = estimated cost of a solution through n"

```graphviz[width=60%]
digraph route_heuristic {
    bgcolor="transparent";
    pad="0.15";
    rankdir=LR;
    nodesep=0.40;
    ranksep=0.55;

    node [shape=circle, style="filled", penwidth=1.4,
          fontname="Helvetica", fontsize=11, width=0.55, fixedsize=true];
    edge [color="#A3B1C0", penwidth=1.2, arrowhead=vee, arrowsize=0.7,
          fontname="Helvetica", fontsize=10, fontcolor="#7B8794"];

    Start [label="Start", fillcolor="#A9DDB0", color="#4F9A5C", fontcolor="#1F4E2E"];
    N     [label="n",     fillcolor="#C7ECF0", color="#7CC6D0", fontcolor="#1F4E56"];
    Goal  [label="Goal",  fillcolor="#9CC4F2", color="#3C6FB0", fontcolor="#1F4E79"];

    Start -> N [label=" g(n) "];
    N -> Goal [label="  h*(n), true  "];
    N -> Goal [label="  h(n), estimate of h*(n)  ", style=dashed,
               color="#C0455B", fontcolor="#C0455B", constraint=false];

    fontname="Helvetica";
    fontsize=10;
    fontcolor="#7B8794";
}
```

- @Example@: route for a car between two city using a map
  - $h(n)$ = straight-line distance from city $n$ to the destination
  - The heuristic is that straight-line and the actual road distance are related, and
    no road is shorter than the straight line

* Greedy Best-First Search

- @Definition@: **greedy best-first search** expands the node with the lowest
  estimated distance to the goal
  $$
  f(n) = h(n)
  $$
  - The cost already paid $g(n)$ is discarded

- @Pros@: expanding the node that looks closest drives the search at the goal
  - E.g., with straight-line $h$ on a road map, it follows the corridor
    pointing at the destination

- @Cons@: ignoring $g$ costs both _optimality_ and _completeness_
  - Without an explored set it can oscillate forever between two states that
    both look promising

- @Counterexample@: two routes to the same goal
  - Route A heads straight at the goal, then follows a long detour
  - Route B starts away from the goal, then runs straight to it
  - Greedy commits to A, because $h$ is smaller after the first step

- @Problem@: an evaluation function built only from $h$ throws away information
  the search already holds for free

* A* Search

- @Definition@: **A\* search** is best-first search ordered by the estimated
  cost of a _complete_ solution passing through $n$
  $$
  f(n) = g(n) + h(n)
  $$
  - $g(n)$: cost of the cheapest path reaching $n$ from the initial state
  - $h(n)$: estimated cost of the cheapest path from $n$ to a goal

```graphviz[width=60%]
digraph route_heuristic {
    bgcolor="transparent";
    pad="0.15";
    rankdir=LR;
    nodesep=0.40;
    ranksep=0.55;

    node [shape=circle, style="filled", penwidth=1.4,
          fontname="Helvetica", fontsize=11, width=0.55, fixedsize=true];
    edge [color="#A3B1C0", penwidth=1.2, arrowhead=vee, arrowsize=0.7,
          fontname="Helvetica", fontsize=10, fontcolor="#7B8794"];

    Start [label="Start", fillcolor="#A9DDB0", color="#4F9A5C", fontcolor="#1F4E2E"];
    N     [label="n",     fillcolor="#C7ECF0", color="#7CC6D0", fontcolor="#1F4E56"];
    Goal  [label="Goal",  fillcolor="#9CC4F2", color="#3C6FB0", fontcolor="#1F4E79"];

    Start -> N [label=" g(n) "];
    N -> Goal [label="  h(n)  "];
    N -> Goal [label="  h*(n), estimate of h(n)  ", style=dashed,
               color="#C0455B", fontcolor="#C0455B", constraint=false];

    fontname="Helvetica";
    fontsize=10;
    fontcolor="#7B8794";
}
```

- @Intuition@: greedy search minimizes the future, uniform-cost search
  minimizes the past, and A* minimizes their sum
  - A* has the same formulation as uniform-cost search, replacing
    $f(n) = g(n)$ with $f(n) = g(n) + h(n)$

- @Remark@: the goal test fires on _expansion_
  - A frontier node with lower $f$ can still overtake a goal already generated

- @Remark@: A* generalizes other algorithms
  - $h \equiv 0$ recovers uniform-cost search
  - $g \equiv 0$ recovers greedy best-first search

* Admissibility and Consistency

// TODO(gp): explain and add intuition

- @Definition@: $h$ is **admissible** if it never overestimates the cost to
  reach the goal at every node $n$
  $$
  0 \leq h(n) \leq h^*(n)
  $$
  - $h^*(n)$ is the true cheapest cost from $n$ to a goal (unknown)
  - _Intuition_: an admissible $h$ is _optimistic_ since $f(n)$ never overestimates the
    true cost of a solution through $n$

- @Definition@: $h$ is **consistent** if, for every node $n$ and every successor
  $n' = Result(n, a)$
  $$
  h(n) \leq Cost(n, a, n') + h(n')
  $$
  - Aka "monotone"
  - _Intuition_: consistency is the triangle inequality on $n$, $n'$, and the goal:
    no side of a triangle is longer than the sum of the other two

- @Proposition@: consistency implies admissibility, but not the reverse
  - If a route from $n$ to the goal through $n'$ were cheaper than $h(n)$, then
    $h(n)$ would not be a lower bound
  - E.g., straight-line distance is consistent, and nearly every admissible heuristic
    used in practice is

* Optimality of A*

- @Theorem@: A* is complete and optimal
  - The tree-search version is optimal when $h$ is **admissible**
  - The graph-search version is optimal when $h$ is **consistent**

::: columns
:::: {.column width=50%}
- @Intuition@: A* pops nodes in non-decreasing order of $f$, so it sweeps out
  **contours** of equal $f$ value
  - Every node with $f(n) < C^*$ is expanded
  - No node with $f(n) > C^*$ is ever expanded
  - Nodes with $f(n) = C^*$ may or may not be expanded, depending on
    tie-breaking
::::
:::: {.column width=45%}

