    n1 -> n2;
    n2 -> G;
}
```

::::
:::

* Where Heuristics Come From

- @Question@: how does one come up with a heuristic, ideally automatically?

- @Answer@: kind-of-ad-hoc
  - _Relaxes problem_: approach
  - _Pattern database_: store exact costs of subproblems over part of the state
  - _Inductive learning_: solve many random instances and fit $h(n)$ from features of
    the state
  - _Meta-level search_: learn during the current search, or from past searches,
    which subtrees are unpromising

* Relaxes Problem

- @Definition@: a **relaxed problem** is the original problem with some rules
  removed
  - Its state space is a _supergraph_ of the original one, because removing
    restrictions adds edges
  - Every solution of the original problem is also a solution of the relaxed
    problem

- @Proposition@: the cost of an optimal solution to a relaxed problem is an
  admissible heuristic for the original problem
  - It is an _exact_ cost in the relaxed problem, so it obeys the triangle
    inequality, hence it is also consistent

- @Example@: the **8-puzzle**, where the average random instance takes about
  $22$ steps and $b \approx 3$
  - Let a tile move anywhere: $h_1$ = number of misplaced tiles
  - Let a tile move to any adjacent cell, occupied or not: $h_2$ = sum of
    Manhattan distances
  - At depth $12$, $b^*$ falls from about $2.8$ for iterative deepening to
    $1.5$ with $h_1$ and $1.3$ with $h_2$

- @Remark@: tic-tac-toe and Connect 4 do not fit this pattern
  - They are adversarial games, with no single path cost to a goal for one
    agent to minimize
  - The analogous idea returns as the heuristic evaluation function $Eval(s)$
    used to cut off adversarial search

// TODO(gp): Don't understand what is this
- @Definition@: $h_2$ **dominates** $h_1$ when $h_2(n) \geq h_1(n)$ everywhere
  and both stay admissible
  - A dominating heuristic never expands more nodes, so always take
    $h(n) = \max(h_1(n), h_2(n))$

# Adversarial Search

* Why Games Are Studied in AI

- @Definition@: a **competitive environment** involves two or more agents with
  conflicting goals
  - An increase in utility for one agent implies a decrease for the others
  - This is what makes the situation _adversarial_

- @Motivation@: **games** are good models of competitive real-world problems
  - The _state_ is easy to represent
  - The number of _actions_ is small
  - The outcome of an action follows precise _rules_
  - The search space is _very large_ 

- @Limitations@: some physical games (e.g. American football) have complicated
  descriptions and imprecise rules about which actions are legal

* Modeling Multi-agent Environments

- @Comparison@: several ways to **model competitive multi-agent environments**
  - _Economy_: aggregate a very large number of agents
    - E.g. predict that rising demand lifts prices without modeling any individual
  - _Part of the environment_: treat adversaries as non-determinism
    - Misses that they actively try to defeat us
    - E.g. treating a Connect 4 opponent's move as a random column pick, so a
      forced block is missed as often as a random one
  - _Adversarial game-tree search_: model the opponent as an "optimizer" (which is
    what minimax does)
    - E.g. minimax assumes the opponent always plays the reply that minimizes
      $Max$'s payoff

* Games as Search Problems

- @Assumptions@: consider the following class of games
  - _Two players_ moving in turn
  - _Deterministic_ with _perfect information_, so the environment is fully
    observable
  - _Zero-sum_, so the utility values are always equal and opposite

// TODO(gp): Maybe convert it into a table
- @Definition@: a **game**  is defined by the following components:
  - _Initial state_ $s_0$: where the game starts
  - _Actions_: the legal actions $Actions(s)$ available in state $s$
  - _Transition model_: the state reached $Result(s, a)$ by doing $a$ in $s$
  - _Turn_: $Player(s)$ is the player who has the move in $s$
    - $Max$ moving first or $Min$ moving second
  - _Goal test_: $IsTerminal(s) = true$ when the game is over
  - _Cost_: $Utility(s, p)$ is the payoff of the terminal state $s$ to player $p$
    - Aka "objective function", or "payoff function"

- In practice, a game replaces the _goal test_ and the _path cost_ of a search
  problem

* Games as Search Problems
- @Definition@: a **terminal state** is the game-theory name for a goal state, i.e.,
  when the game is over with one of the player winning or a draw

- @Definition@: a **zero-sum game** has the same total payoff for every instance of
  the game equal to 0:
  $$
  Utility(s, Max) + Utility(s, Min) = 0 \; \forall s: IsTerminal(s)
  $$
  - "Constant-sum" would have been a better name
    - E.g. win $= 1$, loss $= 0$, draw $= 1/2$ for each player

- @Example@: tic-tac-toe and Connect 4 are both _games_ according to the definition
  - Use $\{+1, 0, -1\}$ for win, draw, and loss
  - The game is always from the point of view of $Max$

* AND-OR tree

::: columns
:::: {.column width=60%}
- @Definition@: an **AND-OR tree** generalizes a search tree with two kinds of
  branching, needed when the outcome of a choice is not fully under the agent's
  control
  - **OR node**: the agent picks one action, so one successful child suffices
  - **AND node**: another party (opponent) picks the outcome, so every child
    must lead to success

- @Intuition@:
  - At an `OR` node, _"I choose"_
  - At an `AND` node, _"someone else chooses"_, so the agent must be ready for
    any of their choices

::::
:::: {.column width=40%}
```graphviz
digraph and_or_tree {
    bgcolor="transparent";
    pad="0.15";
    rankdir=TB;
    splines=line;
    nodesep=0.35;
    ranksep=0.45;

    node [shape=box, style="rounded,filled", penwidth=1.3,
          fontname="Helvetica", fontsize=10, margin="0.10,0.07"];
    edge [color="#A3B1C0", penwidth=1.1, arrowhead=vee, arrowsize=0.6,
          fontname="Helvetica", fontsize=9, fontcolor="#7B8794"];

    // green = OR node ("I choose") | coral = AND node ("someone else chooses")
    // solid red = kept in the solution subtree | dashed = not part of it

    Root [label="OR", fillcolor="#A9DDB0", color="#4F9A5C", fontcolor="#1F4E2E"];
    A1   [label="action 1", fillcolor="#9CC4F2", color="#3C6FB0", fontcolor="#1F4E79"];
    A2   [label="action 2", fillcolor="#9CC4F2", color="#3C6FB0", fontcolor="#1F4E79"];
    M1   [label="AND", fillcolor="#F6C6C6", color="#D98C8C", fontcolor="#6B2A2A"];
    L1   [label="goal", fillcolor="#C7ECF0", color="#7CC6D0", fontcolor="#1F4E56"];
    L2   [label="goal", fillcolor="#C7ECF0", color="#7CC6D0", fontcolor="#1F4E56"];
    L3   [label="goal", fillcolor="#C7ECF0", color="#7CC6D0", fontcolor="#1F4E56"];

    Root -> A1 [color="#C0455B", penwidth=1.6];
    Root -> A2 [style=dashed];
    A1 -> M1 [color="#C0455B", penwidth=1.6];
    M1 -> L1 [color="#C0455B", penwidth=1.6];
    M1 -> L2 [color="#C0455B", penwidth=1.6];
    M1 -> L3 [color="#C0455B", penwidth=1.6];
}
```

::::
:::

- @Comparison@: a search tree has only `OR` nodes, since the agent alone picks every
  move

- @Key idea@: a solution of an AND-OR tree is a **subtree**, not a path
  - Keep one branch at each `OR` node
  - Keep every branch at each `AND` node
  - Every leaf of the subtree is a goal (terminal) state

* Game Trees

::: columns
:::: {.column width=50%}
- @Definition@: a **game tree** has game states as nodes and moves as edges, running
  down to the terminal states (same as a _search tree_)
  - It is defined by $s_0$, $Actions$, and $Result$
  - It is often too large to build explicitly, so it is only ever considered
    implicitly

- @Intuition@: a game tree is the `AND-OR` tree of the game
  - $Max$'s turns are `OR` nodes: $Max$ _chooses_ one action
  - $Min$'s turns are `AND` nodes: $Max$ must survive _every_ reply $Min$
    could pick
  - A solution is therefore not a path but a **contingent strategy**: a reply
    to every move the opponent can make

::::
:::: {.column width=50%}

// TODO(gp): This needs to be improved

```graphviz
digraph game_tree {
    bgcolor="transparent";
    pad="0.15";
    ranksep=0.40;
    nodesep=0.18;

    node [shape=box, style="rounded,filled", penwidth=1.6,
          fontname="Helvetica", fontsize=11, margin="0.13,0.07",
          height=0.34];
    edge [color="#A3B1C0", penwidth=1.2, arrowhead=none];

    // orange = MAX node | cyan = MIN node | blue = leaf value
    // red = path actually played under optimal play

    root [label="MAX = 0", fillcolor="#FFC98A", color="#D98E2B", fontcolor="#6B4517"];

    m1 [label="MIN = 0",  fillcolor="#C7ECF0", color="#7CC6D0", fontcolor="#1F4E56"];
    m2 [label="MIN = -1", fillcolor="#C7ECF0", color="#7CC6D0", fontcolor="#1F4E56"];
    m3 [label="MIN = -1", fillcolor="#C7ECF0", color="#7CC6D0", fontcolor="#1F4E56"];

    l1 [label="+1", fillcolor="#9CC4F2", color="#3C6FB0", fontcolor="#1F4E79"];
    l2 [label="0",  fillcolor="#9CC4F2", color="#3C6FB0", fontcolor="#1F4E79"];
    l3 [label="-1", fillcolor="#9CC4F2", color="#3C6FB0", fontcolor="#1F4E79"];
    l4 [label="0",  fillcolor="#9CC4F2", color="#3C6FB0", fontcolor="#1F4E79"];
    l5 [label="+1", fillcolor="#9CC4F2", color="#3C6FB0", fontcolor="#1F4E79"];
    l6 [label="-1", fillcolor="#9CC4F2", color="#3C6FB0", fontcolor="#1F4E79"];

    root -> m1 [color="#C0455B", penwidth=1.8];
    root -> m2;
    root -> m3;
    m1 -> l1;
    m1 -> l2 [color="#C0455B", penwidth=1.8];
    m2 -> l3;
    m2 -> l4;
    m3 -> l5;
    m3 -> l6;
}
```

::::
:::

- @Definition@: a **ply** is one layer, so one "move" by one player
  - $Max$ layers prefer the largest value, $Min$ layers the smallest
  - The value at each $MIN$ node is the smallest of its leaves, and the root
    value is the largest of the $MIN$ values
    - The red path in the figure shows the line actually played under optimal
      play
  - Each leaf carries the utility of that terminal state from $Max$'s point of
    view

- @Remark@:
  - A _search in the game tree_ (e.g., minimax) is the process of picking a
    value-optimal path through that structure, accounting for what the opponent
    plays at every ply, not just the best move for one side alone
// TODO(gp): Explain the different btw search tree and search in game tree

* The Tic-Tac-Toe Game Tree

::: columns
:::: {.column width=60%}
- @Example@: Tic-Tac-Toe
  - $Max$ has 9 ways to place an `X`, then the players alternate
  - The game ends when a player has three in a row, or all squares are filled
  - $255,168$ complete games, and $549,946$ nodes in total
  - Playing optimally the result is a draw

- @Example@: Connect 4 is the same AND-OR tree as Tic-Tac-Toe
  - Depth $m = 42$ and $b \approx 7$, so about $10^{35}$ move sequences
  - $4.5 \cdot 10^{12}$ distinct legal positions
  - Playing optimally the result is a win for the first player (who must open in
    the center column)
::::
:::: {.column width=40%}

```graphviz
digraph ttt_and_or_tree {
    bgcolor="transparent";
    pad="0.15";
    rankdir=TB;
    splines=line;
    nodesep=0.25;
    ranksep=0.40;

    node [shape=box, style="rounded,filled", penwidth=1.3,
          fontname="Courier", fontsize=9, margin="0.08,0.06"];
    edge [color="#A3B1C0", penwidth=1.1, arrowhead=vee, arrowsize=0.6,
          fontname="Helvetica", fontsize=9, fontcolor="#7B8794"];

    // green = OR node (X to move) | coral = AND node (O to move)
    // blue = board after a move | red = kept in the solution subtree
    // dashed = not explored

    S0 [label="X to move (OR)\n. . .\n. . .\n. . .", fillcolor="#A9DDB0",
        color="#4F9A5C", fontcolor="#1F4E2E"];

    A1 [label="X . .\n. . .\n. . .", fillcolor="#9CC4F2", color="#3C6FB0", fontcolor="#1F4E79"];
    A2 [label=". . .\n. X .\n. . .", fillcolor="#9CC4F2", color="#3C6FB0", fontcolor="#1F4E79"];

    M1 [label="O to move (AND)\nX . .\n. . .\n. . .", fillcolor="#F6C6C6",
        color="#D98C8C", fontcolor="#6B2A2A"];

    L1 [label="X . .\nO . .\n. . .", fillcolor="#C7ECF0", color="#7CC6D0", fontcolor="#1F4E56"];
    L2 [label="X . .\n. O .\n. . .", fillcolor="#C7ECF0", color="#7CC6D0", fontcolor="#1F4E56"];
    L3 [label="X . .\n. . .\nO . .", fillcolor="#C7ECF0", color="#7CC6D0", fontcolor="#1F4E56"];

    S0 -> A1 [color="#C0455B", penwidth=1.6];
    S0 -> A2 [style=dashed];
    A1 -> M1 [color="#C0455B", penwidth=1.6];
    M1 -> L1 [color="#C0455B", penwidth=1.6];
    M1 -> L2 [color="#C0455B", penwidth=1.6];
    M1 -> L3 [color="#C0455B", penwidth=1.6];
}
```
::::
:::

* Minimax

- @Definition@: the **minimax value** of a node is defined recursively
  \begingroup \small
  $$
  Minimax(s) =
  \begin{cases}
  Utility(s, Max) & IsTerminal(s) \\
  \max_{a \in Actions(s)} Minimax(Result(s, a)) & Player(s) = Max \\
  \min_{a \in Actions(s)} Minimax(Result(s, a)) & Player(s) = Min \\
  \end{cases}
  $$
  \endgroup

- @Intuition@:
  - $Minimax(s)$ is the utility for $Max$ assuming both players play optimally from
    $s$ to the end of the game
  - $Max$ MAXimizes the minimax value, $Min$ MINimizes it

- @Algorithm@: compute $Minimax$ by a complete depth-first visit
  1. Recurse down to the terminal states
  2. Propagate the values back up as the recursion unwinds
  3. Return the root action whose child carries the root value

- @Key idea@: the minimax decision maximizes the worst case for $Max$
  - No other strategy does better against an optimal $Min$
  - Against a suboptimal $Min$, $Max$ scores at least $Minimax(s_0)$, and some other
    strategy might score more

* Minimax on a Tic-Tac-Toe Endgame

::: columns
:::: {.column width=32%}
- Cells are numbered $0$ to $8$, left to right and top to bottom

