::::
:::

- @Example@: only cell $5$ blocks O and keeps the column $2$-$5$-$8$ alive, so
  the root value is $0$ and X must play $5$

* Terms in Combinatorial Game Theory

- @Forcing move@: a move that collapses your opponent's option space to one or few
  reasonable replies
  - _Business_: an ultimatum in a negotiation ("accept this term sheet by Friday or
    the offer is off the table")
  - _War_: threaten a critical supply line so the enemy must respond there

- @Killer move@: a move from which the outcome is determined regardless of what the
  opponent does next
  - _Business_: a product launch or acquisition that locks up the market before
    rivals can react
  - _War_: a decisive strike that ends the campaign in one blow

- @Fork@: create two threats at once where the opponent can only counter one
  - _Business_: launch two competing initiatives at once, forcing a rival to split
    resources
  - _War_: attack on two fronts simultaneously, splitting enemy forces

- @Taking initiative / gaining tempo@: force your opponent to react rather than
  execute their own plan
  - _Business_: rapid, continuous feature releases that keep competitors perpetually
    responding to you instead of shipping their own roadmap
  - _War_: a fast moving offensive that keeps the enemy constantly repositioning

* Terms in Combinatorial Game Theory

- @Commitment device@: an action that removes your own future options, making a
  threat or promise credible
  - _Business_: a "money back guarantee" that removes the option to quietly
    underdeliver
  - _War_: "burning the boats" so retreat was impossible

- @Signaling@: conveying information about your intentions or capabilities
  - _Business_: a company announces a large R&D budget increase to signal it intends
    to compete aggressively in a new market
  - _War_: a show of force military exercise near a border to signal resolve without
    direct engagement

- @Brinkmanship@: deliberately escalating risk to force an opponent to back down
  - _Business_: a union credibly threatens a full strike during peak season to force
    concessions at the bargaining table
  - _War_: Cold War nuclear posturing, where each side escalated risk to force the
    other to concede first

* Minimax: Properties and Cost
// From: msml610/lectures_source/Lesson09.9-MonteCarlo_Tree_Search.smd:58 '* Minimax and Alpha-Beta'

- @Properties@: minimax is a complete depth-first visit of the game tree
  - _Complete_ whenever the tree is finite
  - _Optimal_ against an optimal opponent, and no worse against any other
  - _Time_ $O(b^m)$, since every terminal state is examined
  - _Space_ $O(bm)$, from the depth-first recursion

- @Problem@: the time bound makes minimax impractical
  - Connect 4 needs about $10^{35}$ terminal evaluations
  - The growth sits in the _exponent_, so faster machines buy almost nothing

- @Question@: can the exact minimax value be computed without examining the
  entire tree?

- @Key idea@: yes, because a branch can be _proven_ irrelevant before it is
  fully searched
  - The root value cannot depend on a move that one of the players has already
    rejected
    - E.g., if $Max$ already has a move worth $10$, and the first reply
      $Min$ finds in a sibling branch is worth $5$, that branch is worth at
      most $5$ regardless of its remaining children, so $Max$ never enters it

* Alpha-Beta Pruning
// From: msml610/lectures_source/Lesson09.9-MonteCarlo_Tree_Search.smd:58 '* Minimax and Alpha-Beta'

- @Definition@: **pruning** ignores portions of the search tree that make no
  difference to the final choice
  - Alpha-beta pruning returns exactly the minimax value, so it is a smaller
    search and not an approximation

// TODO(gp): Explain better with an example and a plot

- @Intuition@: consider a node $n$ that player $p$ could move to
  - If $p$ has a better choice $m$ anywhere further up the tree, then $n$ is
    never reached in actual play
  - As soon as that is known, $n$ can be pruned

// TODO(gp): Not clear

- @Example@: let $Max$ choose among $B$, $C$, $D$, with the children of $C$
  holding unknown values $x$ and $y$
  \begin{align*}
  & Minimax(A) \\
  & = \max(\min(3, 12, 8), \min(2, x, y), \min(14, 5, 2)) \\
  & = \max(3, \min(2, x, y), 2) \\
  & = 3
  \end{align*}
  - $\min(2, x, y) \leq 2$ whatever $x$ and $y$ are, so both subtrees are
    pruned

- @Key idea@: the value of the root is independent of the pruned subtrees

* Alpha-Beta: Worked Example

::: columns
:::: {.column width=44%}
- $Max$ chooses $a_1$, $a_2$, $a_3$ leading to $B$, $C$, $D$, and the leaves
  are read left to right

- @Example@: the trace
  - $B$: leaves $3, 12, 8$ give $Minimax(B) = 3$, so $\alpha = 3$ at the root
  - $C$: the first leaf is $2$, hence $Minimax(C) \leq 2 \leq \alpha$, and the
    remaining leaves are never generated
  - $D$: leaves $14$ and $5$ leave $Minimax(D) > 3$ possible, and the leaf $2$
    settles $Minimax(D) = 2$

- @Remark@: $2$ of $9$ leaves are skipped and the answer is unchanged
::::
:::: {.column width=50%}

```graphviz
digraph alpha_beta {
    bgcolor="transparent";
    pad="0.15";
    ranksep=0.38;
    nodesep=0.14;

    node [shape=box, style="rounded,filled", penwidth=1.6,
          fontname="Helvetica", fontsize=10, margin="0.12,0.06",
          height=0.32];
    edge [color="#A3B1C0", penwidth=1.2, arrowhead=none];

    r [label="A: MAX = 3", fillcolor="#FFC98A", color="#D98E2B", fontcolor="#6B4517"];

    B [label="B = 3",    fillcolor="#C7ECF0", color="#7CC6D0", fontcolor="#1F4E56"];
    C [label="C <= 2",   fillcolor="#C7ECF0", color="#7CC6D0", fontcolor="#1F4E56"];
    D [label="D = 2",    fillcolor="#C7ECF0", color="#7CC6D0", fontcolor="#1F4E56"];

    b1 [label="3",  fillcolor="#9CC4F2", color="#3C6FB0", fontcolor="#1F4E79"];
    b2 [label="12", fillcolor="#9CC4F2", color="#3C6FB0", fontcolor="#1F4E79"];
    b3 [label="8",  fillcolor="#9CC4F2", color="#3C6FB0", fontcolor="#1F4E79"];
    c1 [label="2",  fillcolor="#9CC4F2", color="#3C6FB0", fontcolor="#1F4E79"];
    c2 [label="x",  fillcolor="#F2F2F2", color="#C7D0DA", fontcolor="#9AA9B8", style="rounded,filled,dashed"];
    c3 [label="y",  fillcolor="#F2F2F2", color="#C7D0DA", fontcolor="#9AA9B8", style="rounded,filled,dashed"];
    d1 [label="14", fillcolor="#9CC4F2", color="#3C6FB0", fontcolor="#1F4E79"];
    d2 [label="5",  fillcolor="#9CC4F2", color="#3C6FB0", fontcolor="#1F4E79"];
    d3 [label="2",  fillcolor="#9CC4F2", color="#3C6FB0", fontcolor="#1F4E79"];

    r -> B [label="a1"];
    r -> C [label="a2"];
    r -> D [label="a3"];
    B -> b1;
    B -> b2;
    B -> b3;
    C -> c1;
    C -> c2 [style=dashed, color="#C7D0DA"];
    C -> c3 [style=dashed, color="#C7D0DA"];
    D -> d1;
    D -> d2;
    D -> d3;
}
```

::::
:::

- @Key idea@: pruning is driven by the best value already guaranteed, so
  finding a strong move early prunes far more

* Alpha-Beta: Worked Example

::: columns
:::: {.column width=44%}
- $Max$ chooses $a_1$, $a_2$, $a_3$ leading to $B$, $C$, $D$, and the leaves
  are read left to right

- @Example@: the trace
  - $B$: leaves $3, 12, 8$ give $Minimax(B) = 3$, so $\alpha = 3$ at the root
  - $C$: the first leaf is $2$, hence $Minimax(C) \leq 2 \leq \alpha$, and the
    remaining leaves are never generated
  - $D$: leaves $14$ and $5$ leave $Minimax(D) > 3$ possible, and the leaf $2$
    settles $Minimax(D) = 2$

- @Remark@: $2$ of $9$ leaves are skipped and the answer is unchanged
